## Enabling Routing in a Standalone App

To add routing capabilities to a standalone Angular application, you must configure providers in `main.ts`.

- Import `provideRouter` from `@angular/router`.
- In the `bootstrapApplication` function call, add a `providers` array.
- Call `provideRouter` within the `providers` array, passing it an array of your application's routes.

```typescript
// main.ts
import { provideRouter } from '@angular/router';
import routeConfig from './app/routes';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideRouter(routeConfig)]
});
```

## Defining and Displaying Routes

Routes are defined as an array of `Route` objects, typically in a separate `routes.ts` file.

- **Route Object**: Each route is an object with properties like `path`, `component`, and `title`.
  - `path`: The URL segment for the route (e.g., `''` for home, `'details'` for a details page).
  - `component`: The component class to display for that route.
  - `title`: The string to set as the browser page title.
- **Router Outlet**: The `<router-outlet>` directive in your `app.component.html` acts as a placeholder where Angular renders the component for the current route.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> For routing features like `<router-outlet>` and `routerLink` to work, you must import `RouterModule` into the `imports` array of any component that uses them.

## Navigating with Parameterized Routes

Parameterized routes allow you to pass dynamic data, like an item ID, through the URL.

### 1. Create a Dynamic Link

Use the **`routerLink`** directive on an anchor tag. To pass dynamic values, use property binding (`[]`).

```html
<!-- housing-location.component.html -->
<a [routerLink]="['/details', housingLocation.id]">Learn More</a>
```
- The value is an array where the first element is the static path and subsequent elements are dynamic parameters.

### 2. Define the Parameterized Route Path

In `routes.ts`, use a colon (`:`) to mark a segment of the path as a parameter.

```typescript
// routes.ts
{
  path: 'details/:id',
  component: DetailsComponent,
  title: 'Details Page'
}
```

### 3. Access the Parameter in the Component

Use the **`ActivatedRoute`** service to get information about the current route, including its parameters.

- Inject `ActivatedRoute` into your component.
- Access the parameters via `route.snapshot.params`.

```typescript
// details.component.ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router';

export class DetailsComponent {
  route: ActivatedRoute = inject(ActivatedRoute);
  housingLocationId = -1;

  constructor() {
    const id = this.route.snapshot.params['id'];
    this.housingLocationId = Number(id);
  }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> Route parameters are always returned as strings. Use functions like `Number()` to convert them to the expected data type.
